today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://lmsys.org/blog/2025-09-25-gb200-part-2/\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://www.lmsys.org/blog/2025-09-25-gb200-part-2/; Total lines: 88\nL0:   * cite0†Projects L1:   * cite1†Blog L2:   * cite2†About L3:   * cite3†Donations L4:   * cite4†Contact L5: \nL6: contact@lmsys.org\nL7: \nL8:   * cite0†Projects L9:   * cite1†Blog L10:   * cite2†About L11:   * cite3†Donations L12:   * cite4†Contact L13: \nL14: contact@lmsys.org\nL15: \nL16: cite1†‹ Back to Blog L17: \nL18: cite1†‹ Back to Blog L19: \nL20: Contents\nL21: \nL22:   * cite5†Methods L23:   * cite6†Experiments L24:   * cite7†End-to-end Performance L25:   * cite8†Zoom into Low-precision Kernels L26:   * cite9†Accuracy L27:   * cite10†Future Work L28:   * cite11†Acknowledgement L29: # Deploying DeepSeek on GB200 NVL72 with PD and Large Scale EP (Part II): 3.8x Prefill, 4.8x Decode Throughput\nL30: \nL31: The SGLang Team September 25, 2025\nL32: The GB200 NVL72 is one of the most powerful hardware for deep learning. In this blog post, we share our progress after our cite12†previous blog post†lmsys.org to optimize the inference performance of DeepSeek V3/R1 with FP8 attention, NVFP4 MoE, large-scale expert parallelism, prefill-decode disaggregation, and various other optimizations.\nL33: When using FP8 attention and NVFP4 MoE, SGLang achieved 26,156 input and 13,386 output tokens per second per GPU for prefill and decode, respectively, on DeepSeek V3/R1 for 2000-token input sequences, which is a 3.8x and 4.8x speedup compared to cite13†H100 settings†lmsys.org . Even with traditional BF16 attention and FP8 MoE, SGLang still achieves 18,471 input and 9,087 output tokens per second. Reproduction instructions can be found cite14†here†github.com .\nL34: Highlights\nL35:   * SGLang achieves 26,156 input and 13,386 output tokens per second per NVIDIA Blackwell GPU for prefill and decode, respectively, on DeepSeek V3/R1 for 2000-token input sequences, which is a 3.8x and 4.8x speedup compared to H100 settings.\n"
